scientists on the spanish island la  palma are monitoring the air for toxic  gases being released as lava from the  erupting volcano empties into the  atlantic the reaction is sending acidic  vapor into the air for now winds are  carrying the gases out to sea but  islanders the locals they're worried  that that situation could change  the steaming lava makes for an  impressive spectacle but people are  being warned to keep their distance  since wednesday night lava with a  temperature of around 1 000 degrees  celsius has been flowing into the sea  the good news is that the plumes of  toxic gases created when molten lava  hits salty water have so far been blown  out to sea  but experts remain vigilant  the gases will have to be monitored we  have to bear in mind that each liter of  sea water contains 35 grams of sodium  chloride so large quantities are now  evaporating from the sea forming  hydrochloric acid which is harmful for  people's health  a curfew therefore remains in place in  several locations there's growing  concern because the wind that has so far  been fanning the toxic steam out to sea  has now weakened  residents have been urged to stay  indoors  i can't smell anything and i don't feel  any different the ash is annoying  because of the breeze that's blowing  but for others it's tough  many of the banana plantations that are  key to the economy of the canary islands  have been destroyed the lava has also  blocked access to the plantations that  remain  it's a total disaster yesterday we  couldn't water the plants it's the same  today  we have to drive almost halfway around  the island to get to work but right now  we have no other choice  the lava that is still spewing from the  cumbre vieja volcano has engulfed around  470 hectares of land and destroyed  nearly 800 houses in its path out to sea  but as the lava starts to cool in the  sea it's creating new land as a result  the island of la palma has already grown  by about 10 hectares  our correspondent nicole riss is on la  palma tonight  it's here behind me where the lava is  finally meeting the sea and this creates  an enormous amount of possibly very  toxic clouds which is also why residents  close to this area in a perimeter of 3.5  kilometers are currently confined to  their homes they're advised not to leave  their houses to seal their doors and  windows and to wait for more information  coming from scientists and local  authorities and it's this very lava that  we see coming into the sea right here  that has been making its way from the  cumbria vieja volcano for over a week  now destroying more than 815 buildings a  path of destruction for sure and we got  a little taste of that just earlier when  we tried out a different position and it  suddenly started hailing down on us  small volcanic pebbles pebble-like  stones that were coming out at us it was  a very  interesting situation to say the least  but it gives you an idea of how  dangerous it can be the closer you get  to this volcano some locals even said  that the kumbreva volcano is like a  monster and that it destroys everything  that's in its way and this monster may  rumble on for a few weeks or even for a  few months
 

